AR (AutoRegression)
------------------
| Item      | Description                                               |
| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Formula   | Current Value = Previous Values                           |
| Example   | Predict today's stock price using yesterday's stock price |
| Real Life | Temperature forecasting                                   |


Example:

| Day       | Price |
| --------- | ----- |
| Monday    | 100   |
| Tuesday   | 105   |
| Wednesday | ?     |

AR uses Monday and Tuesday to predict Wednesday.

===========

MA (Moving Average)

| Item      | Description                                                                    |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Formula   | Current Value = Previous Errors                                                |
| Example   | If previous forecasts were wrong, use those errors to improve future forecasts |
| Real Life | Sales forecasting                                                              |

Example:

| Forecast | Actual | Error |
| -------- | ------ | ----- |
| 100      | 105    | +5    |
| 110      | 108    | -2    |


MA learns from these errors.

==============

ARMA

| Item     | Description               |
| -------- | ------------------------- |
| Combines | AR + MA                   |
| Uses     | Past values + Past errors |
| Example  | Stock price prediction    |


Example:

Today's Price
      =
Past Prices + Past Errors

=============

ARIMA

| Item    | Description              |
| ------- | ------------------------ |
| AR      | Past values              |
| I       | Differencing             |
| MA      | Past errors              |
| Example | Stock market forecasting |


Workflow:

Original Data
      ↓
Differencing
      ↓
Stationary Data
      ↓
ARIMA

Example:

| Year | Sales |
| ---- | ----- |
| 2020 | 100   |
| 2021 | 150   |
| 2022 | 200   |

ARIMA removes trend first
===============

SARIMA

| Item                | Description                 |
| ------------------- | --------------------------- |
| ARIMA + Seasonality | Captures repeating patterns |
| Example             | Ice cream sales             |

Example:

| Month    | Sales |
| -------- | ----- |
| June     | High  |
| July     | High  |
| December | Low   |

SARIMA learns seasonal cycles.

===========

SARIMAX

| Item                        | Description                                         |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| SARIMA + External Variables |                                                     |
| Example                     | Sales forecast using seasonality and advertisements |


Example:

| Month | Sales | Ads |
| ----- | ----- | --- |
| Jan   | 100   | 10  |
| Feb   | 150   | 20  |

Ads affect sales.

============

VAR

| Item                                  | Description                  |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Forecasts multiple variables together |                              |
| Example                               | Stock Price + Trading Volume |


Example:

| Day | Price | Volume |
| --- | ----- | ------ |
| Mon | 100   | 5000   |
| Tue | 102   | 5200   |

Both influence each other.

============

VARMA

| Item                 | Description                              |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| VAR + Error Modeling |                                          |
| Example              | Stock Price + Volume + Prediction Errors |


Used when multiple variables interact and contain error patterns.

==========

VARMAX

| Item                       | Description                          |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| VARMA + External Variables |                                      |
| Example                    | Stock Price + Volume + Interest Rate |

| Day | Price | Volume | Interest Rate |
| --- | ----- | ------ | ------------- |
| Mon | 100   | 5000   | 5%            |
| Tue | 102   | 5200   | 5.2%          |


External factors influence forecasting.

